L0045: 
L0046: classification_tags.csv
L0047: 
L0048: 
L0049: The classification_tags.csv file contains several parameters that should be helpful for users. All the categorical tags are non-exclusive,
L0050: and simply mean a given subscene contained some of that characteristic. For example, snow-covered mountain tops with forests in the
L0051: lower valleys, would be tagged with forest/jungle, snow/ice and hills/mountains. Similarly multiple cloud types can exist within the same
L0052: image. All tags were determined by visual inspection of both the image and, for SURFACE TYPE, other high-resolution imagery of the
L0053: area (e.g. BingMaps and Google Earth). Objective classifications of this kind are not possible, and all our classifications here are made
L0054: using subjective judgement. We describe each column (grouped into broad categories) as follows:
L0055: 
L0056: 
L0057: 
L0058: GENERAL INFORMATION:
L0059: 
L0060:      scene: string, Sentinel-2 product ID.
L0061:      difficulty: int, Subjective measure from 1->5 for difficulty of annotation.
L0062:           1. Near perfect
L0063:           2. Very good
L0064:           3. Mostly good
L0065:           4. Possibly some small errors
L0066:           5. Possibly large errors
L0067:      annotator: string, Which of the two annotators, A or B, created the mask used in masks/ (note that alt_masks/ may contain the
L0068:      other annotator's mask, if both labelled that subscene).
L0069:      shadows_marked: boolean, if 0 then no shadow markings were possible. If 1, shadows were marked where present.


=== PAGE 3 ===
L0001:      clear_percent: float, 0 -> 100 for percentage of pixels marked CLEAR
L0002:      cloud_percent: float, 0 -> 100 for percentage of pixels marked CLOUD
L0003:      shadow_percent: float, 0 -> 100 for percentage of pixels marked CLOUD_SHADOW
L0004:      dataset: string, referring to whether subscene was part of CALIBRATION, MAIN, or VALIDATION labelling stages (described in
L0005:      Section "Annotation Strategy")
L0006: 
L0007: 
L0008: 
L0009: SURFACE TYPES:
L0010: 
L0011:      forest/jungle: boolean, dense tree cover
L0012:      snow/ice: boolean, any snow or ice covering either land or water
L0013:      agricultural: boolean, farmlands or pastures
L0014:      urban/developed: boolean, human structures, conurbations, villages, industrial sites. Not including solitary roads.
L0015:      coastal: boolean, coastlines, specifically. (Not just water near to land, but the division between land and water itself)
L0016:      hills/mountains: boolean, noticeable hilly terrain
L0017:      desert/barren: boolean, completely arid, or very sparsely vegetated, drylands.
L0018:      shrublands/plains: boolean, somewhat sparsely vegetated areas, or non-forested areas without obvious signs or agriculture.
L0019:      wetland/bog/marsh: boolean, areas with large amounts of standing water (although )
L0020:      open_water: boolean, extended bodies of water, such as large lakes that continue beyond the image bounds, or the sea.
L0021:      enclosed_water: boolean, lakes, large rivers or contained areas of sea-water (e.g. fjords)
L0022: 
L0023: 
L0024: 
L0025: CLOUD THICKNESS:
L0026: 
L0027:      thin: boolean, any cloud where some surface colour/signal can be seen through it (not including at the edges of thick clouds, to
L0028:      avoid all thick tags being accompanied by thin ones)
L0029:      thick: boolean, any cloud where no surface can be seen through it (except possibly at its edges).
L0030: 
L0031: 
L0032: 
L0033: RELATIVE CLOUD HEIGHT:
L0034: 
L0035:      low: boolean, any cloud which has no obvious sign of high-altitude (high reflectance in the Cirrus (B10) band)
L0036:      high: boolean, any cloud with noticable reflectance in the Cirrus (B10) band
L0037: 
L0038: 
L0039: 
L0040: CLOUD EXTENT:
L0041: 
L0042: 
L0043:      isolated: boolean, clouds which are separated from one another, such that there are no areas of continuous cloud across the
L0044:      majority of the subscene.
L0045:      extended: boolean, clouds which span continuously across a majority of a subscene.
L0046: 
L0047: 
L0048: 
L0049: CLOUD TYPE:
L0050: 
L0051:      cumulus: boolean, rounded, clumpy and separate clouds.
L0052:      cumulonimbus: boolean, clouds with very large, steep, vertical profiles, reaching up very high.
L0053:      altocumulus/stratocumulus: boolean, most general cloud type, denoting any clouds that form periodic cells. Could be high or low,
L0054:      and thick or thin.
L0055:      cirrus: boolean, thin, extended clouds that have high reflectance in the Cirrus (B10) band. Sometimes almost invisible or very faint
L0056:      in any other band.
L0057:      haze/fog: boolean, low-lying, textureless, thin cloud. Often distinguishable because it looks similar to cirrus, but with no reflectance
L0058:      in B10.
L0059:      ice_clouds: boolean, any cloud that has a noticeable icy signature, seen as reddish-orange in the false-colour B1 (red), B11
L0060:      (green), B12 (blue)
L0061:      contrails: boolean, exhaust trails left by planes.


=== PAGE 4 ===
L0001: subscenes
L0002: 
L0003: 
L0004: Each subscene is a 1022-by-1022-by-13 numpy array, with Top of Atmosphere reflectance values held as float32 numbers. These are
L0005: taken directly from the Sentinel-2 L1C product that they share their name with, cropped randomly (but resampled until an area without
L0006: any no-data values is found). Bands that are not at 20m are resampled to 20m using bilinear interpolation. Users who wish to use the
L0007: bands at their original resolutions will need to download the L1C products and use the shapefiles provided to extract the area of the
L0008: mask.
L0009: 
L0010: The order of Sentinel-2 bands in the numpy array's 3rd dimension is in numerical order, with band 8A coming between bands 8 and 9. All
L0011: values are found by dividing the original L1C integer values by 10'000, to retrieve the reflectances, as advised in the Product
L0012: Specification for Sentinel-2 L1C. Please note that many values go above 1, because apparent reflectance can be greater than 1 if a
L0013: surface is at an angle which receives more light than is possible on a surface normal to the viewing angle.
L0014: 
L0015: 
L0016: 
L0017: masks
L0018: 
L0019: 
L0020: Each mask are a 1022-by-1022-by-3 numpy array, with boolean one-hot encoding (each pixel has exactly one True value across the last
L0021: dimension). The class order in the last dimension is: CLEAR, CLOUD, CLOUD_SHADOW. Even if shadow markings were not possible
L0022: for a given subscene, the third channel is still included. The masks are named using the corresponding product IDs of the scenes.
L0023: 
L0024: 
L0025: 
L0026: shapefiles
L0027: 
L0028: 
L0029: An Esri shapefile for each scene which describes the polygon of the extracted subscene. These can be used to extract the original band
L0030: data from the Sentinel-2 L1C scenes if users need them.
L0031: 
L0032: 
L0033: 
L0034: thumbnails
L0035: 
L0036: 
L0037: A set of downsampled .png images showing the subscenes, plus a small area around them. Not to be used for any processing, they are
L0038: included to give a simple way to browse the data.
L0039: 
L0040: 
L0041: 
L0042: alt_masks
L0043: 
L0044: 
L0045: For scenes which were annotated by both annotators (10 in CALIBRATION and 50 in VALIDATION—see Appendix B for more details
L0046: about this process) the other annotators mask is provided for completeness. This can be used to verify the statistical tests we performed
L0047: using them, or as backup masks should you spot one that you would prefer to use for any reason. The selection was random, so they
L0048: are just as valid as those found in the mask folder.


